// @description Resets ONLYOFFICE image uploads whose encrypted-file header names a path.
// @patches ONLYOFFICE Document Server 5.1.5 to 5.6.2
// @cve CVE-2021-3199
// @severity critical
// @tags onlyoffice, path-traversal, rce
// @knob port  The port to guard.
// @knob strict  Set to 1 to reset compressed and chunked request bodies too.
//
// Fixed in Document Server 5.6.3 (server 5.6.3.2).
//
// POST /upload/DOC/USER/INDEX stores an image for a document. With JWT on, a
// token that marks the document as encrypted makes the server accept a body
// that begins `ENCRYPTED;`, and take the file's extension from what lies
// between that and the next semicolon. The affected versions put it into the
// storage path as it comes, so `ENCRYPTED;/../../..NAME;` writes the rest of
// the body to any file the server can write, its own executables among them.
// The fix accepts only an extension on the configured list. This asks for an
// extension that is one:
//
//   onlyoffice_encrypted  a line that begins `ENCRYPTED;` and has anything
//                         but letters and digits before the next semicolon
//   onlyoffice_encoding   with `strict` set to 1: a request with a
//                         Content-Encoding or Transfer-Encoding header
//
// Read from the source of server 5.6.3.1 (fileuploaderservice.js, server.js)
// and of the fix, not from a running Document Server:
//   - the marker is compared exactly, in upper case, at the first byte of
//     the body, so the first rule keeps its case while the rest of the
//     policy ignores it
//   - the route's body parser inflates a gzip or deflate body, and a chunked
//     body can be cut inside the marker; in either the marker is not on the
//     wire as the server will read it, and the first rule does not see it.
//     A browser sends neither, but curl and other API clients do send
//     chunked bodies, so resetting them is left to the operator: set
//     `strict` to 1 where only browsers use the server. Without it this
//     stops the request as published and not one that has been compressed
//   - the traversal is in the body, not in the URL or the token, so the path
//     is not part of the match; the older route /uploadold/ and anything
//     else on the port is held to the same rule
//
// Ordinary use passes: an image upload, and an encrypted one from the
// desktop editors (`ENCRYPTED;png;`). The cost of the first rule: a text
// body with a line that starts `ENCRYPTED;` and then punctuation is reset.
//
// The attacker needs a token signed with the server's JWT secret; the
// versions concerned shipped with a well-known default. Change it.
//
// Plaintext HTTP/1 only: put it behind the TLS terminator, in front of the
// Document Server's own nginx.
nocase;
// Reassembly follows port 80 only. With the `port` knob set to anything
// else, packets are judged one at a time and a signature split across two is
// missed: to guard another port properly, change the number here as well.
reassemble 80;
regex encrypted = "(?-i)\nENCRYPTED;[A-Za-z0-9]*[^A-Za-z0-9;]";
regex encoding = "\n(content|transfer)-encoding[ \t]*:";
filter {
  if (proto != 6) decide pass;
  if (dport != knob("port") ?? 80) decide pass;
  let strict = knob("strict") ?? 0;
  mask m = scan(payload, 4096);
  if (any(m, encrypted)) decide rst("onlyoffice_encrypted", 0);
  if (any(m, encoding)) {
    if (strict != 0) decide rst("onlyoffice_encoding", 0);
  }
  decide pass;
}
